The presentation is not where you explain your work. It's where you transfer your belief in it.

There is a profound difference between a deck that *reports* and a deck that *argues*. You are not filing a brief. You are making a case. Every slide, every transition, every word you choose in a live demo is in service of one thing: **getting the room to feel what you felt when you figured it out**.

**VoxPops** are underused and undervalued. Real users saying real things — unprompted, unpolished — land harder than any slide you'll ever make. The voice of someone who actually cares about the problem cuts through every layer of stakeholder scepticism.

Present live when you can. There is no substitute for being in the room, reading the room, and responding to the room.
